# the correct source code to be generated into ScalaTarget, and I would prefer that Consumer not appear in
# drake or json as part of the type definition." On 2fcf58e (623 / 44 green).
#
# ONE CONCEPT: `pattern (Knowledge -> Unit)`, `action (RhsContext -> Unit)` in Rule / RuleType (elements and
# Rule's factory); Knowledge and RhsContext are draco.rete wrap types, so nothing in the slot is the host's.
# A pattern is APPLIED — `X.ruleType.pattern(k)` — in the engine's knowledge block and eleven tests.
#
# WHY CONSUMER WAS THERE (Dev): Evrete is Java, called from Scala, and `execute` does not accept Scala
# idioms. MEASURED with scalac from the exported classpath (scratchpad probe2): a function VALUE at
# `RuleBuilder.execute` is refused ("found RhsContext => Unit, required java.util.function.Consumer"); a
# lambda LITERAL `execute(action(_))` compiles. The generated rule already relied on that conversion for
# `action` itself. So the ScalaTarget names the interface at exactly one call, in the template.
#
# ENGINE: ruleBody's two declared types, `execute (action(_))`, the knowledge block's `pattern(k)`, and
# `ruleFrameworkImports` loses the Consumer import (the externalTypeImports "Consumer" row stays: dead for
# this, and pre-existing). Rule.scala / RuleType.scala regenerated by the compiled engine from the re-parsed
